# State access — selectors for reads, action creators for writes

## Context

`app/src/actions.ts` owns action payload shapes; `app/src/selectors.ts` owns
derived reads (`visibleTasks`, `remainingCount`). Consumers such as
`app/src/index.ts` never build action objects by hand and never filter
`state.tasks` inline — otherwise the same logic drifts into several places.

## Rule

- Writes: consumers call an action creator from `app/src/actions.ts` and pass
  the result to `store.dispatch(...)` — e.g.
  `store.dispatch(toggleTask(id))`. NEVER write
  `store.dispatch({ type: "task/toggled", payload: { id } })` outside
  `actions.ts`.
- Every new `Action` variant gets a matching creator in `actions.ts`, named in
  camelCase after the intent (`addTask` for `"task/added"`), with the return
  type annotated as `Action`.
- Reads: derived/filtered data comes from a selector in
  `app/src/selectors.ts`. NEVER inline `state.tasks.filter(...)` /
  `.find(...)` in consumers or tests-of-behavior — add or reuse a selector.
- Selectors are pure `(state: AppState, ...args) => T`: no dispatching, no
  mutation, no caching side effects.
- Action creators contain no logic beyond building the payload (no id
  generation, no validation) — pass values in.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rn "dispatch({" app/src` → zero matches (all dispatches go through
   creators).
2. Every `type:` string literal in `app/src` appears only in `types.ts`,
   `reducer.ts` and `actions.ts`:
   `grep -rn "\"task/\|\"filter/" app/src` confirms it.
3. `grep -rnE "state\.tasks\.(filter|find|some|reduce)" app/src` → matches
   only inside `selectors.ts` and `reducer.ts`.
4. `cd app && npm run typecheck && npm test` — green.

